{#- The head both pages share: the theme script, the favicon, the fonts,
    the stylesheet and the parameter colour tokens. Reads `page_title`,
    `theme_default`, `css` and `param_colors` from the context. -#}
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
{#- Resolves the theme before the stylesheet parses, so a dark report never
    flashes light. Order: the viewer's saved choice, then the configured
    default. All three choices are stored explicitly — an absent key, not
    "auto", is what means "use the default", so a viewer's System beats a
    report configured dark. Plain script, no Alpine: it runs before the bundle
    exists. It owns the whole mechanism — storage, the choice → theme rule and
    the follow-the-system listener — and hands app.js `window.__REPORT_THEME__`
    with the resolved `choice` and a `set` the toggle calls. -#}
<script>
(function () {
  var root = document.documentElement;
  var key = 'pytest-given-theme';
  var darkScheme = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)');
  var stored = null;
  try { stored = localStorage.getItem(key); } catch (error) { /* no storage: this page only */ }
  var theme = {
    choice: (stored === 'light' || stored === 'dark' || stored === 'auto') ? stored : root.getAttribute('data-theme-default'),
    set: function (choice) {
      theme.choice = choice;
      apply();
      try { localStorage.setItem(key, choice); } catch (error) { /* no storage: this page only */ }
    }
  };
  function apply() {
    var dark = theme.choice === 'dark' || (theme.choice === 'auto' && darkScheme.matches);
    root.setAttribute('data-theme', dark ? 'dark' : 'light');
  }
  apply();
  darkScheme.addEventListener('change', apply);
  window.__REPORT_THEME__ = theme;
})();
</script>
<title>{{ page_title }}</title>
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="{{ data_url('logo.svg') }}">
<style>
{#- Fontsource variable latin subsets (OFL 1.1, see THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES),
    inlined so the report stays one file. Upright only: nothing in the report
    is set in italic, so no face is embedded for the browser to synthesise. -#}
@font-face { font-family: "Source Sans 3"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 200 900; font-display: swap; src: url("{{ data_url('fonts/source-sans-3-latin-wght-normal.woff2') }}") format("woff2-variations"); }
@font-face { font-family: "Source Code Pro"; font-style: normal; font-weight: 200 900; font-display: swap; src: url("{{ data_url('fonts/source-code-pro-latin-wght-normal.woff2') }}") format("woff2-variations"); }
{{ css }}
{# One token and one rule per parametrize column, generated for this report's
   column count. Like every other colour, the token is set once per theme and
   the rule only reads it. See report/palette.py for how the ring is derived;
   styles.css holds only the properties every column shares. The `th` half of
   the selector is there to outrank `.param-table th`, which sets a color of
   its own. #}
{%- for light, dark in param_colors %}
:root { --param-color-{{ loop.index0 }}: {{ light }}; }
[data-theme="dark"] { --param-color-{{ loop.index0 }}: {{ dark }}; }
.param-color-{{ loop.index0 }}, th.param-color-{{ loop.index0 }} { color: var(--param-color-{{ loop.index0 }}); }
{%- endfor %}
</style>
